 "intro": "The forests and marshes of northern Germany in September. You are Publius Quinctilius Varus, governor of Germany, with three legions (XVII, XVIII and XIX), auxiliaries and camp followers: perhaps 15,000 legionaries and 4,500 auxiliaries. Your trusted officer Arminius, a Cheruscan noble and Roman citizen, has secretly united the tribes against you.",
 "debrief": "In reality, Segestes warned Varus that Arminius was plotting, but Varus did not believe him. Arminius invented a revolt and suggested a shorter route through unfamiliar country, then left to gather his men. In the forest the column stretched to 15 or 20 km, and warriors attacked along its length in heavy rain. Varus built a fortified camp; the Romans then marched by night and were led into a trap at the foot of Kalkriese Hill, where the road ran about 100 metres wide between woods and a great bog, blocked by a turf wall. Attacks on the wall failed; Numonius Vala rode off with the cavalry and was killed; Varus killed himself. Some 15,000 to 20,000 Romans died, and the eagles of three legions were lost; their numbers were never used again. The site at Kalkriese is supported by excavation. Arresting Arminius, the known road and burning the wagons are what-ifs.",
